
# This file controls what Groovy files in this directory get run.
# The format is the same as the Unix crontab file.
# To see docs run on Linux or Mac:  man 5 crontab
#
# This crontab file, as well as any of the Groovy files the cron system runs
# can be changed on a running system.  There is no need to rebuild the system.
#
# In particular, each line in this file specifies when particular Groovy files get run.
# Each Groovy file must exist in this directory (backend/CronTasks).
#
# Format of this file.
#
# Blank lines or those beginning with "#" are ignored comment lines.
# Each non-comment line specifies all of the parameters for a single Groovy command.
#
# The  format of a cron command is very much the V7 standard, with a num‐
# ber of upward-compatible extensions.  Each line has five time and  date
# fields,  followed by a command, followed by a newline character (’\n’).
# The fields may be separated by spaces or tabs.
#
# Commands are executed when the minute, hour, and month of
# year fields match the current time, and when at least one of the two
# day fields (day of month, or day of week) match the current  time
# Cron once every minute.
#
# The time and date fields are:
#
#        field          allowed values
#        -----          --------------
#        minute         0-59
#        hour           0-23
#        day of month   1-31
#        month          1-12
#        day of week    0-7 (0 or 7 is Sun, or use names)
#
# A field may be an asterisk (*), which always stands for ‘‘first-last’’.
#
# Ranges of numbers are allowed.  Ranges are two numbers separated with a
# hyphen.   The  specified  range is inclusive.  For example, 8-11 for an
# ‘‘hours’’ entry specifies execution at hours 8, 9, 10 and 11.
#
# Lists are allowed.  A list is a set of numbers (or ranges) separated by
# commas.  Examples: ‘‘1,2,5,9’’, ‘‘0-4,8-12’’.

# Uncomment the following line to run the EveryMinute.groovy file
#* * * * * EveryMinute

# Tier 3 memory maintenance ("sleep") job --- OFF by default.
# Two independently-gated passes, both using the configured LLM:
#   - consolidation/dedup (CONSOLIDATION_ENABLED): merge near-identical
#     memories into one canonical fact, supersede the copies.
#   - conflict resolution (CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_ENABLED): among same-topic
#     memories that contradict, keep the current one, supersede the stale.
# Both only soft-delete (recoverable), and skip any cluster holding a
# keep=Y memory.
#
# Cost: each enabled pass makes AT MOST CONSOLIDATION_MAX_GROUPS (default
# 25) small LLM calls, and ZERO calls when there are no clusters to act on
# (the steady state once the store is tidy).  Choose the model via
# LLM_MODEL --- point it at a cheap one.
#
# To enable: (1) uncomment the line below (runs nightly at 03:00 --- adjust
# as you like), (2) set the LLM_* keys plus CONSOLIDATION_ENABLED=true
# and/or CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_ENABLED=true in application.ini.  With the
# LLM unset, or both passes disabled, the fired job is a cheap no-op.
#0 3 * * * Consolidate

# Tier 4 phase 2 relation-graph extraction --- OFF by default.
# When enabled it asks the LLM to extract (subject, predicate, object)
# triples from memories into the relation graph that 'query_relations'
# traverses.  Each memory is extracted at most once; a run makes AT MOST
# GRAPH_EXTRACTION_MAX_MEMORIES (default 25) small LLM calls and ZERO once
# the store is fully extracted.
#
# To enable: (1) uncomment the line below (runs nightly at 03:30), (2) set
# GRAPH_EXTRACTION_ENABLED=true and the LLM_* keys in application.ini.
#30 3 * * * ExtractRelations

